<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<BES xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="BES.xsd">
	<Task>
		<Title>Scan with YARA-X Signatures - macOS</Title>
		<Description><![CDATA[<P>Scans this computer for whatever software the deployed YARA signatures detect, and records the version of each. It uses <A href="https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x">YARA-X</A> with every rule file in the YARA engine signatures directory, <CODE>__BESData/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara/signatures</CODE>, in one pass; the version is read from strings compiled into the binaries, so nothing is run. Each signature file is loaded into its own YARA namespace, named after the file's base name without its extension (<CODE>example_detect.yar</CODE> becomes namespace <CODE>example_detect</CODE>), so a rule identifier only has to be unique inside its own file; two signature files with the same base name are an error. Run &quot;Deploy YARA-X Signatures - Universal&quot; first: this task is not relevant until at least one signature file is present.</P><P>Results: one line per product found in a file, &quot;signature&lt;TAB&gt;path&lt;TAB&gt;product&lt;TAB&gt;version&quot;, where signature is the signature file whose rule matched and product is a short label taken from the name of the rule that matched - the text before its first underscore, lowercased - so which products appear depends on which signatures are deployed (one file can produce more than one line when several rules match it; the version is &quot;unknown&quot; for a match whose rule carries no readable version string), in __BESData/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara/results/yara_results_runtimes.text. A second file, yara_results_signatures.text in the same folder, holds &quot;signature&lt;TAB&gt;count&quot; for every signature that was loaded, in signature-name order, so a signature that matched nothing is still listed with a count of 0. The analysis &quot;YARA-X Scan Results - Universal&quot; reads both files; activate it to report the matches. The task stays relevant whenever a signature file is present and no scan is running, so how often a rescan happens is set by how the action is scheduled (a reapplying or scheduled action), not by the age of the results.</P><P>Runs as root. The BigFix agent needs Full Disk Access (MDM PPPC profile) to read protected folders; without it they are skipped. The read-only system volume, cloud-drive folders, files of 512 MB or more and non-executable file types are skipped.</P><P>The scan runs in the background (started with run, so the agent stays free for other actions) from __BESData/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara/work and reads the whole disk, which can take 10-30 minutes or more. The yr engine is kept in the sibling bin folder and reused by later scans. The action completes when the scan starts; the results file appears when it finishes, and its console output goes to __BESData/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara/results/yara_scan_runtimes.log. While a scan is running the task is not relevant, so a second one is not started.</P>]]></Description>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[mac of operating system]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[version of operating system >= "10.12"]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[not in proxy agent context]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[exists files whose(name of it as lowercase ends with ".yar" OR name of it as lowercase ends with ".yara") of folders "signatures" of folders "yara" of folders "__MitigationToolkit" of folders "__Global" of data folders of client]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[not exists files whose(name of it = "scan.running" AND (now - modification time of it) < 12*hour) of folders "work" of folders "yara" of folders "__MitigationToolkit" of folders "__Global" of data folders of client]]></Relevance>
		<Category></Category>
		<DownloadSize>9476144</DownloadSize>
		<Source>Internal</Source>
		<SourceID>jgstew</SourceID>
		<SourceReleaseDate>2026-11-17</SourceReleaseDate>
		<SourceSeverity></SourceSeverity>
		<CVENames></CVENames>
		<SANSID></SANSID>
		<MIMEField>
			<Name>x-fixlet-modification-time</Name>
			<Value>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</Value>
		</MIMEField>
		<Domain>BESC</Domain>
		<DefaultAction ID="Action1">
			<Description>
				<PreLink>Click </PreLink>
				<Link>here</Link>
				<PostLink> to scan with the deployed YARA-X signatures.</PostLink>
			</Description>
			<ActionScript MIMEType="application/x-Fixlet-Windows-Shell"><![CDATA[
// Scan with the deployed YARA-X signatures and record the versions found - macOS
// Generated by YARA/make_bigfix_content.py from bash/yara_x_scan_macos.sh in
// https://github.com/jgstew/tools - change the script there and regenerate.

// yr (YARA-X CLI) release archive for this OS/CPU; the script still checks its SHA256
begin prefetch block
	if {architecture of operating system = "arm64"}
		add prefetch item name=yara-x-v1.22.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz sha1=16b30fd7d7613bd0172bc197d785f1143c34f2b9 size=8421133 url=https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x/releases/download/v1.22.0/yara-x-v1.22.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz sha256=79d596da5bf02205de63a1d7844a964daebc7293fa36fcb7da46c3e3c7567229
	endif
	if {architecture of operating system != "arm64"}
		add prefetch item name=yara-x-v1.22.0-x86_64-apple-darwin.tar.gz sha1=560ace0fca8543195a71d52545bef248e92854e4 size=9476144 url=https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x/releases/download/v1.22.0/yara-x-v1.22.0-x86_64-apple-darwin.tar.gz sha256=d059da4b91c7d7b43f6f50174a7739e3362b51f349f2ba8b33d46e3334bb3300
	endif
end prefetch block

// The YARA engine layout under the client data folder (the __BESData folder),
// created by "Deploy YARA-X Signatures - Universal":
//   bin         yr, kept between scans
//   signatures  the .yar rule files this scan uses
//   results     the scan output read by the analysis
//   work        this scan's scripts, archive and scan.running marker
parameter "engine_folder" = "{pathname of data folders of client}/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara"
parameter "bin_folder" = "{parameter "engine_folder"}/bin"
parameter "signatures_folder" = "{parameter "engine_folder"}/signatures"
parameter "results_folder" = "{parameter "engine_folder"}/results"
// The scan runs in the background from here, not __Download: the client clears
// __Download when the action ends, and the scan outlives the action
parameter "temp_folder" = "{parameter "engine_folder"}/work"
parameter "results_file" = "yara_results_runtimes.text"
// Per-signature counts written beside the summary; both names are read by the
// analysis "YARA-X Scan Results - Universal" - do not rename either
parameter "signatures_file" = "yara_results_signatures.text"

// The deploy task creates bin and results; recreated here only if they are missing
// so a scan still runs after someone cleans the folder out
if {not exists folders "bin" of folders "yara" of folders "__MitigationToolkit" of folders "__Global" of data folders of client}
	folder create "{parameter "bin_folder"}"
endif
if {not exists folders "results" of folders "yara" of folders "__MitigationToolkit" of folders "__Global" of data folders of client}
	folder create "{parameter "results_folder"}"
endif
folder delete "{parameter "temp_folder"}"
folder create "{parameter "temp_folder"}"
copy "__Download/{name of file whose(name of it starts with "yara-x-v") of folder "__Download" of client folder of current site}" "{parameter "temp_folder"}/{name of file whose(name of it starts with "yara-x-v") of folder "__Download" of client folder of current site}"

// The scan script; every left curly brace in it is doubled, BigFix's escape for relevance substitution
delete __createfile
createfile until _END_OF_SCRIPT_
#!/usr/bin/env bash
#### Usage: ./yara_x_scan_macos.sh -r RULES_PATH [-t TARGET_PATH] [-o OUTPUT_DIR] [-b BIN_DIR] [-f text|json|ndjson] [-k] [-a YR_ARCHIVE] [-m MAX_MB] [-T THREADS] [-x DIR]... [-A]
#
# Self-contained YARA-X scan for macOS:
#   1. downloads the YARA-X CLI (yr) release for this CPU architecture
#   2. verifies the SHA256 of the download against a pinned value
#   3. unpacks it into BIN_DIR (default: a temporary folder removed on exit)
#   4. scans TARGET_PATH (recursively) with every YARA rule file in RULES_PATH,
#      which is a .yar/.yara file or a directory of them, so every deployed
#      signature is applied in one scan. Each rule file is loaded into its own
#      YARA namespace, named after the file's base name without its extension
#      (signatures/php_detect.yar -> namespace php_detect), so a rule
#      identifier only has to be unique inside its own file. Two rule files
#      with the same base name are a usage error (exit 2). The scan stays on
#      TARGET_PATH's volume: other mounts (DMGs, network shares, external
#      disks, autofs, Time Machine snapshots) are skipped
#   5. writes results + a log to OUTPUT_DIR; for text format:
#        yara_results_<host>_<time>.raw.text  full yr output (rules, meta, strings)
#        yara_results_<host>_<time>.text
#            "signature<TAB>path<TAB>product<TAB>version", one line per product
#            found in a file. signature is the rule file (namespace) whose rule
#            matched, worked out from a rule-identifier -> signature map built
#            by reading the rule files, not from yr's output
#        yara_results_signatures_<host>_<time>.text
#            "signature<TAB>count", one line for every signature loaded, in
#            signature-name order, where count is how many lines of the summary
#            above came from that signature. A signature that matched nothing
#            is still listed, with count 0
#
#        product comes from the matched rule's name - the text before its
#        first underscore, lowercased (php, node, apache, java, ...); an
#        Electron framework gets a node and an electron line, and
#        Apache_HTTP2_* gives mod_http2. Files matched with no
#        version string, e.g. Linux PHP extensions or Node addons, are
#        listed with version "unknown". Files whose paths contain control
#        characters (newline, tab, ...) are scanned as copies and shown as
#        bash $'...' quoted paths (e.g. $'/opt/new\nline/php').
#
# This script carries no YARA rules of its own: -r RULES_PATH is required and
# the rule files live outside it (in BigFix, deployed to the engine signatures
# directory by "Deploy YARA-X Signatures - Universal"). The rule files that
# come from YARA/ are YARA/php/php_detect.yar, YARA/node/node_detect.yar and
# YARA/apache/apache_detect.yar (each with the header check from
# YARA/is_executable.yar). The deploy task also ships rule files from
# elsewhere, such as java_detect.yar, which are maintained with that task
# rather than in YARA/. The versions.awk filter below is still copied in
# from YARA/versions.awk by YARA/sync_rules.py: edit it there, then run
#   python3 YARA/sync_rules.py
# (CI fails if the copy here is out of date). The rest of the script is edited
# here. YARA/make_bigfix_content.py embeds the whole script in its BigFix
# task, so regenerate it after any change.
#
# Run with sudo (and give the terminal / agent Full Disk Access) for a full
# scan; otherwise protected files are logged as unreadable and skipped.
#
# Options:
#   -r RULES_PATH   required: a .yar/.yara rule file, or a directory of them
#                   (searched recursively, in full-path order). Each file is
#                   loaded into its own namespace named after the file's base
#                   name without its extension, and every match is attributed
#                   to that signature in the results. A path that does not
#                   exist, a directory with no rule file in it, or two rule
#                   files that would share a namespace, is a usage error:
#                   scanning with no rules would waste hours and find nothing,
#                   and a duplicate namespace would hide one file's rules
#   -t TARGET_PATH  file or folder to scan            (default: / = whole local boot drive)
#   -o OUTPUT_DIR   folder to write results into      (default: ./yara_results)
#   -b BIN_DIR      unpack yr into this folder and leave it there, so later
#                   scans reuse it (default: the temporary folder, removed on exit)
#   -f FORMAT       yr output format: text|json|ndjson (default: text)
#   -k              keep the downloaded yr archive in OUTPUT_DIR, to reuse
#                   later with -a OUTPUT_DIR (its SHA256 is checked again)
#   -a YR_ARCHIVE   use a local yr release archive instead of downloading: the
#                   file, or a folder holding it under its release name (e.g. a
#                   BigFix prefetch folder); its SHA256 is still checked
#   -m MAX_MB       skip files of MAX_MB MB or larger, 0 = no limit
#                   (default: 512, or 0 with -A)
#   -T THREADS      yr scan threads                   (default: 1)
#   -x DIR          also skip this folder; repeat for more (a find -path
#                   pattern such as '*/node_modules' works too)
#   -A              no default skips: scan everything listed below as skipped
#                   by default, except empty files and cloud files; -x still
#                   applies
#   -h              show this help
#
# Skipped by default (none of these can hold a likely PHP, Node.js or Apache
# install; each skip is reported while running, files over the size cap are
# listed in the log). To scan just one of these folders, target it (e.g.
# -t /usr/bin) instead of using -A:
#   - files of 512 MB or more: PHP builds are ~20 MB, the largest known
#     (FrankenPHP, static PHP in Go) ~180 MB, node 8.x-25.x 33-127 MB,
#     Electron frameworks 125-350 MB; this cuts VM disks, Docker.raw, model
#     weights, media
#   - on macOS 12+ (no bundled PHP): /System, /bin, /sbin, /usr except
#     /usr/local, /Library/Apple - the read-only sealed system volume. Apple's
#     bundled Apache (/usr/sbin/httpd, /usr/libexec/apache2) is still scanned
#   - images, media, archives, documents, fonts, web/source/text files, C
#     headers, Xcode/SDK data (.swiftmodule, .tbd, ...), logs and databases:
#     the rules only match executables (by header); files with no extension,
#     .dylib, .so, .dll, .exe, .a, .o are kept
#   - empty files
#   - ~/Library/CloudStorage, ~/Library/Mobile Documents (iCloud), and
#     online-only (dataless) files anywhere, e.g. iCloud "Desktop & Documents":
#     reading them would download them
# A skip whose folder contains TARGET_PATH is turned off automatically, and
# none apply when TARGET_PATH is a single file.
#
# find and yr run at low priority (nice 10) and yr uses 1 thread (-T 2 for faster scans), so a
# full scan doesn't take over the machine.
#
# Exit codes: 0 = scan ran, no matches; 1 = scan ran, matches found;
#             2 = usage error; 3 = download/verify/unpack error; 4 = scan error
#
# Release: https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x/releases/tag/v1.22.0
# SHA256 values below are the GitHub-computed asset digests for v1.22.0.
# Related: https://github.com/jgstew/tools/tree/master/YARA

set -euo pipefail

# Treat file names as bytes: in a UTF-8 locale find's [[:cntrl:]] also matches
# U+0080-U+009F, which bash 3.2's printf %q then splits into invalid UTF-8 that
# makes awk fail. In the C locale those names are ordinary valid UTF-8 for yr.
export LC_ALL=C

YARAX_VERSION="1.22.0"
YARAX_BASE_URL="https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x/releases/download/v${{YARAX_VERSION}"
SHA256_AARCH64="79d596da5bf02205de63a1d7844a964daebc7293fa36fcb7da46c3e3c7567229"
SHA256_X86_64="d059da4b91c7d7b43f6f50174a7739e3362b51f349f2ba8b33d46e3334bb3300"

TARGET_PATH="/"
OUTPUT_DIR="$PWD/yara_results"
# Required: a .yar/.yara file, or a directory of them (walked recursively by yr)
RULES_PATH=""
# Where yr is unpacked. Empty = a 'bin' folder in the temporary work directory,
# removed with it. Give a lasting folder to reuse the engine.
BIN_DIR=""
OUTPUT_FORMAT="text"
KEEP_ARCHIVE=0
YR_ARCHIVE=""
MAX_MB=""
MAX_MB_SET=0
THREADS=1
EXCLUDE_DIRS=()
NO_SKIPS=0

# Extensions of files that can't start with an executable header. Compared
# case-insensitively with the text after the last "." of the file name. The second
# line was measured on a Mac (no executables among them): Xcode/SDK, unified
# logs, Chromium/app resources and SQLite/LMDB side files, ~half of the files
# left after the first line. Not .a: macOS static libraries can be universal binaries.
NONBINARY_EXTS='jpg jpeg png gif heic tif tiff bmp webp ico icns svg svgz mov mp4 m4v avi mkv mp3 m4a aac wav aiff caf flac pdf zip gz tgz xz bz2 zst 7z rar dmg pkg xip iso jar whl docx xlsx pptx doc xls ppt pages numbers key txt md rst json js mjs cjs ts tsx jsx map css scss html htm xml plist strings nib car py pyc rb php java class go rs c cc cpp h hpp m swift yml yaml toml ini csv tsv log sqlite db sqlite-wal sqlite-shm ttf otf woff woff2 mo po lock sum'  # codespell:ignore caf
NONBINARY_EXTS="$NONBINARY_EXTS swiftmodule swiftdoc swiftinterface swiftsourceinfo tbd modulemap apinotes pcm metallib tracev3 bnnsir pak fxf data bom db-wal db-shm mdb"

usage() {{
  awk 'NR > 1 && /^# Release:/ {{ exit } NR > 1 {{ sub(/^# ?/, ""); print }' "$0"
}

log() {{
  echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] $*" >&2
}

while getopts ":t:o:r:b:f:ka:m:T:x:Ah" opt; do
  case "$opt" in
    t) TARGET_PATH="$OPTARG" ;;
    o) OUTPUT_DIR="$OPTARG" ;;
    r) RULES_PATH="$OPTARG" ;;
    b) BIN_DIR="$OPTARG" ;;
    f) OUTPUT_FORMAT="$OPTARG" ;;
    k) KEEP_ARCHIVE=1 ;;
    a) YR_ARCHIVE="$OPTARG" ;;
    m) MAX_MB="$OPTARG"; MAX_MB_SET=1 ;;
    T) THREADS="$OPTARG" ;;
    x) [ "$OPTARG" = / ] && EXCLUDE_DIRS+=( / ) || EXCLUDE_DIRS+=( "${{OPTARG%/}" ) ;;
    A) NO_SKIPS=1 ;;
    h) usage; exit 0 ;;
    :) echo "Option -$OPTARG requires an argument." >&2; usage; exit 2 ;;
    \?) echo "Unknown option: -$OPTARG" >&2; usage; exit 2 ;;
  esac
done

case "$OUTPUT_FORMAT" in
  text|json|ndjson) ;;
  *) echo "Invalid format '$OUTPUT_FORMAT' (use text, json or ndjson)." >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

# -A lifts the size cap too, unless -m is given
if [ "$MAX_MB_SET" -eq 0 ]; then
  [ "$NO_SKIPS" -eq 1 ] && MAX_MB=0 || MAX_MB=512
fi
SKIP_SEALED=$((1 - NO_SKIPS))
SKIP_NONBINARY=$((1 - NO_SKIPS))
# Not turned off by -A: reading online-only files would download them
SKIP_CLOUD=1

case "$MAX_MB" in
  ''|*[!0-9]*) echo "Invalid -m '$MAX_MB' (whole number of MB, 0 = no limit)." >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

case "$THREADS" in
  ''|*[!0-9]*|0) echo "Invalid -T '$THREADS' (number of threads, 1 or more)." >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

if [ "$(uname -s)" != "Darwin" ]; then
  echo "This script is for macOS only." >&2
  exit 2
fi

# ---- rules ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The rules are deployed separately, so a missing or empty set is a usage
# error: scanning every file with no rules would waste hours and find nothing.
if [ -z "$RULES_PATH" ]; then
  echo "No -r RULES_PATH given (a .yar/.yara file, or a directory of them)." >&2
  exit 2
fi
if [ ! -e "$RULES_PATH" ]; then
  echo "-r RULES_PATH does not exist: $RULES_PATH" >&2
  exit 2
fi
# Each rule file is loaded into its own namespace instead of handing yr the
# whole directory, so rule identifiers only have to be unique within a file.
# The list is sorted by full path so the namespaces, the attribution of an
# identifier that appears in two files, and the results are all deterministic.
RULE_FILES=()
if [ -d "$RULES_PATH" ]; then
  while IFS= read -r rule_file; do
    [ -n "$rule_file" ] || continue
    RULE_FILES+=( "$rule_file" )
  done < <(find "$RULES_PATH" -type f \( -iname '*.yar' -o -iname '*.yara' \) 2>/dev/null | sort)
else
  RULE_FILES=( "$RULES_PATH" )
fi
RULE_COUNT="${{#RULE_FILES[@]}"
if [ "$RULE_COUNT" -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "No .yar/.yara rule files in -r RULES_PATH: $RULES_PATH" >&2
  exit 2
fi

# NAMESPACE:PATH arguments for yr, one per rule file. bash 3.2 (stock macOS)
# has no associative arrays, so the namespaces are kept in a parallel array and
# the duplicate check is a linear scan - the signature set is a handful of files.
RULE_NAMESPACES=()
RULE_ARGS=()
rule_i=0
while [ "$rule_i" -lt "$RULE_COUNT" ]; do
  rule_file="${{RULE_FILES[$rule_i]}"
  rule_ns="${{rule_file##*/}"
  rule_ns="${{rule_ns%.*}"
  if [ -z "$rule_ns" ]; then
    echo "Cannot derive a signature namespace from rule file: $rule_file" >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  rule_j=0
  while [ "$rule_j" -lt "$rule_i" ]; do
    if [ "${{RULE_NAMESPACES[$rule_j]}" = "$rule_ns" ]; then
      echo "Two rule files share the signature namespace '$rule_ns':" >&2
      echo "  ${{RULE_FILES[$rule_j]}" >&2
      echo "  $rule_file" >&2
      echo "Rename one of them: each signature file needs a unique base name." >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    rule_j=$((rule_j + 1))
  done
  RULE_NAMESPACES+=( "$rule_ns" )
  RULE_ARGS+=( "${{rule_ns}:${{rule_file}" )
  rule_i=$((rule_i + 1))
done

if [ ! -e "$TARGET_PATH" ]; then
  echo "Target path does not exist: $TARGET_PATH" >&2
  exit 2
fi

# uname -m reports arm64 on Apple Silicon, but the release asset is named aarch64
case "$(uname -m)" in
  arm64|aarch64) ARCH="aarch64"; EXPECTED_SHA256="$SHA256_AARCH64" ;;
  x86_64)        ARCH="x86_64";  EXPECTED_SHA256="$SHA256_X86_64" ;;
  *) echo "Unsupported architecture: $(uname -m)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

ASSET_NAME="yara-x-v${{YARAX_VERSION}-${{ARCH}-apple-darwin.tar.gz"
ASSET_URL="${{YARAX_BASE_URL}/${{ASSET_NAME}"

if ! WORK_DIR="$(mktemp -d -t yara_x_scan)"; then
  echo "Cannot create a temporary folder" >&2
  exit 3
fi
cleanup() {{ rm -rf "$WORK_DIR"; }
trap cleanup EXIT

if ! mkdir -p "$OUTPUT_DIR"; then
  echo "Cannot create output folder: $OUTPUT_DIR" >&2
  exit 2
fi
TIMESTAMP="$(date '+%Y%m%d_%H%M%S')"
HOST_SHORT="$(hostname -s)"
RESULT_BASE="${{OUTPUT_DIR}/yara_results_${{HOST_SHORT}_${{TIMESTAMP}"
SUMMARY_FILE=""
# Per-signature counts, written beside the summary whenever the summary is
# written (text format only) and read by the analysis
SIGNATURE_FILE=""
if [ "$OUTPUT_FORMAT" = "text" ]; then
  RESULT_FILE="${{RESULT_BASE}.raw.text"
  SUMMARY_FILE="${{RESULT_BASE}.text"
  SIGNATURE_FILE="${{OUTPUT_DIR}/yara_results_signatures_${{HOST_SHORT}_${{TIMESTAMP}.text"
else
  RESULT_FILE="${{RESULT_BASE}.${{OUTPUT_FORMAT}"
fi
LOG_FILE="${{OUTPUT_DIR}/yara_scan_${{HOST_SHORT}_${{TIMESTAMP}.log"

# ---- download -------------------------------------------------------------
if [ -n "$YR_ARCHIVE" ]; then
  [ -d "$YR_ARCHIVE" ] && YR_ARCHIVE="${{YR_ARCHIVE%/}/${{ASSET_NAME}"
  if [ ! -f "$YR_ARCHIVE" ]; then
    log "ERROR: yr archive not found: $YR_ARCHIVE"
    exit 3
  fi
  log "Using local archive $YR_ARCHIVE"
  if ! cp "$YR_ARCHIVE" "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}"; then
    log "ERROR: cannot read $YR_ARCHIVE"
    exit 3
  fi
else
  log "Downloading $ASSET_URL"
  if ! curl --fail --silent --show-error --location \
       --retry 3 --retry-delay 2 --connect-timeout 30 \
       --output "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" "$ASSET_URL"; then
    log "ERROR: download failed"
    exit 3
  fi
fi

# ---- verify ---------------------------------------------------------------
ACTUAL_SHA256="$(shasum -a 256 "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" | awk '{{print $1}')"
if [ "$ACTUAL_SHA256" != "$EXPECTED_SHA256" ]; then
  log "ERROR: SHA256 mismatch for $ASSET_NAME"
  log "  expected: $EXPECTED_SHA256"
  log "  actual:   $ACTUAL_SHA256"
  exit 3
fi
log "SHA256 verified: $ACTUAL_SHA256"

if [ "$KEEP_ARCHIVE" -eq 1 ]; then
  if [ -n "$YR_ARCHIVE" ]; then
    log "Not keeping the archive (-k): it is already local at $YR_ARCHIVE"
  # A failed copy only costs a download next time, so it doesn't stop the scan
  elif cp "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" "${{OUTPUT_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}"; then
    log "Kept ${{OUTPUT_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME} (reuse with -a $OUTPUT_DIR)"
  else
    log "WARNING: could not keep the archive in $OUTPUT_DIR"
  fi
fi

# ---- unpack ---------------------------------------------------------------
# With -b, yr is unpacked into that folder and stays there for later scans;
# without it, into the work folder, which cleanup() removes on exit
YR_BIN_DIR="${{BIN_DIR:-${{WORK_DIR}/bin}"
if ! mkdir -p "$YR_BIN_DIR"; then
  log "ERROR: cannot create the bin folder: $YR_BIN_DIR"
  exit 3
fi
if ! tar -xzf "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" -C "$YR_BIN_DIR"; then
  log "ERROR: failed to unpack $ASSET_NAME"
  exit 3
fi
YR="${{YR_BIN_DIR}/yr"
if [ ! -x "$YR" ]; then
  log "ERROR: yr binary not found in archive"
  exit 3
fi
# curl does not set the quarantine flag, but strip it defensively so Gatekeeper won't block yr
xattr -d com.apple.quarantine "$YR" 2>/dev/null || true
log "Unpacked $("$YR" --version)"

# ---- rules ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Each rule file is passed to yr as NAMESPACE:PATH (RULE_ARGS, built above), so
# the rules of one signature file cannot collide with another's. Logged so the
# result file says which signatures produced it.
if [ -d "$RULES_PATH" ]; then
  log "Rules: every .yar/.yara file in $RULES_PATH ($RULE_COUNT file(s)), one namespace each"
else
  log "Rules: $RULES_PATH"
fi
for rule_arg in "${{RULE_ARGS[@]}"; do
  log "  signature ${{rule_arg%%:*} <- ${{rule_arg#*:}"
done

# Rule identifier -> signature map, read back by versions.awk to say which
# signature produced each summary line. Built by reading the rule files, not
# from yr's output: yr's --print-namespace layout alongside --print-meta /
# --print-strings is not documented, so parsing the rule files is the
# deterministic option. The list of signatures is written out separately
# because a signature file with no rules in it still has to be counted.
RULE_MAP_FILE="${{WORK_DIR}/rule_map.txt"
SIGNATURE_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/signature_list.txt"
RULE_MAP_AWK="${{WORK_DIR}/rule_map.awk"
cat > "$RULE_MAP_AWK" <<'AWK_RULE_MAP'
# Read YARA rule files and print "<RULE IDENTIFIER><TAB><signature>", where the
# signature is the file's base name without its extension. Files are read in
# sorted order, so an identifier defined in more than one file is attributed to
# the first of them and the rest only produce a warning on stderr.
# A rule may carry the "global" and/or "private" qualifiers, in either order,
# so up to two of them are allowed and stripped. Missing one would cost that
# rule its attribution silently (it would fall back to signature "unknown").
# The qualifier groups are repeated rather than written as an interval
# ({{0,2}): BSD awk does not support interval expressions. The brace is doubled
# because this comment lives inside a BigFix createfile body.
FNR == 1 {{
  ns = FILENAME
  sub(/.*\//, "", ns)
  sub(/\.[^.]*$/, "", ns)
}

/^[ \t]*((global|private)[ \t]+)?((global|private)[ \t]+)?rule[ \t]+[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/ {{
  name = $0
  sub(/^[ \t]+/, "", name)
  sub(/^(global|private)[ \t]+/, "", name)
  sub(/^(global|private)[ \t]+/, "", name)
  sub(/^rule[ \t]+/, "", name)
  sub(/[^A-Za-z0-9_].*$/, "", name)
  if (name == "") next
  if (name in owner) {{
    if (ownerfile[name] != FILENAME)
      printf "WARNING: rule %s is defined in both %s and %s; attributing it to signature %s\n", \
             name, ownerfile[name], FILENAME, owner[name] > "/dev/stderr"
    next
  }
  owner[name] = ns
  ownerfile[name] = FILENAME
  printf "%s\t%s\n", name, ns
}
AWK_RULE_MAP
awk -f "$RULE_MAP_AWK" "${{RULE_FILES[@]}" >"$RULE_MAP_FILE" 2>>"$LOG_FILE" || {{
  log "ERROR: cannot read the rule files to build the signature map"
  exit 2
}
printf '%s\n' "${{RULE_NAMESPACES[@]}" | sort -u >"$SIGNATURE_LIST"
log "Signature map: $(wc -l <"$RULE_MAP_FILE" | tr -d ' ') rule identifier(s) over $(wc -l <"$SIGNATURE_LIST" | tr -d ' ') signature(s)"

# ---- embedded version extractor (text format) -----------------------------
# Copied from YARA/versions.awk by YARA/sync_rules.py - edit there.
VERSIONS_AWK="${{WORK_DIR}/versions.awk"
cat > "$VERSIONS_AWK" <<'AWK_SCRIPT'
# Turn `yara -s` / `yr scan -s` output of the deployed signature files into
# "signature<TAB>path<TAB>product<TAB>version", one line per product found in a
# file. The product is read from the matched rule's name, so a signature file
# added later needs no change here (today: php, node, electron, apache,
# mod_http2, java):
#   yr scan -s -r php_detect:php/php_detect.yar node_detect:node/node_detect.yar apache_detect:apache/apache_detect.yar /opt 2>/dev/null | awk -f versions.awk -v MAPFILE=rule_map.txt rule_map.txt - | sort -u
# signature is the rule file (YARA namespace) whose rule matched. It comes from
# MAPFILE, a "<RULE IDENTIFIER><TAB><signature>" map built by reading the rule
# files, which must be the first input file and is named again with -v MAPFILE
# so an empty map cannot be mistaken for the scan output. A rule identifier
# that is not in the map is reported with signature "unknown". If yr instead
# prints the rule name already qualified with its namespace, that namespace is
# used directly and the map is not needed for that line (see the rule line
# block below).
# Handles paths with spaces and the UTF-16 ProductVersion match ($pv), which
# yara prints with escaped NULs (8\x00.\x002\x00...). Matched files with no
# version string (e.g. Linux PHP extensions, Node addons) get "unknown".
# Versions: php from $ver / $pv; node from $ver (node.js/v...), or from $rel
# (the release URL) when no $ver matched; electron from $electron, so an
# Electron framework gets a node and an electron line; apache from $ver
# (Apache/...); mod_http2 from $_module_ver (a candidate, see apache_detect.yar).
# A rule whose matched strings carry none of those names (e.g. java_detect's
# $ver_modern) still gets its line, with version "unknown".

# rule identifier -> signature map, read before the scan output
FILENAME == MAPFILE {{
  i = index($0, "\t")
  if (i > 0) sigof[substr($0, 1, i - 1)] = substr($0, i + 1)
  next
}

# rule line: "<RULE> <path>", or with yr --print-meta "<RULE> [meta...] <path>".
# NOT dead code: the rules are now loaded as NAMESPACE:PATH, and yr may print
# the rule name qualified with that namespace ("php_detect:PHP_Runtime_Version").
# Without the optional prefix here, every rule line would stop matching and the
# scan would report nothing found - a false negative that looks like a clean
# machine. The prefix is stripped off the identifier token only, never off the
# whole line: a path's first colon would be eaten with it.
# Any rule identifier is accepted, not a fixed list of products, so a signature
# file added later is summarised instead of being silently dropped. yr's string
# lines ("0x1a2b:$ver: ...") cannot match: an identifier may not start with a
# digit, and the pattern is anchored.
/^([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:)?[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*_/ {{
  rid = $1
  rns = ""
  if (rid ~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/) {{
    rns = rid
    sub(/:.*$/, "", rns)
    sub(/^[^:]*:/, "", rid)
  }
  # The product is the rule name's prefix - the text before its first
  # underscore, lowercased: PHP_Runtime_Version gives php, a Java_* rule gives
  # java, and a name with no underscore gives the whole name. One exception:
  # Apache_HTTP2_* is the mod_http2 module, not httpd itself.
  prod = tolower(rid)
  sub(/_.*$/, "", prod)
  if (rid ~ /^Apache_HTTP2_/) prod = "mod_http2"
  # A namespace yr printed is authoritative; the map is the fallback for a
  # bare identifier
  sig = (rns != "") ? rns : ((rid in sigof) ? sigof[rid] : "unknown")
  file = $0; sub(/^[^ ]+ /, "", file); sub(/^\[[^]]*\] /, "", file); seen[sig SUBSEP file SUBSEP prod] = 1; next
}

# string line: "0xOFFSET:$id: <matched text>"
/:\$(ver|pv|rel|electron|_module_ver): / {{
  v = $0; gsub(/\\x00/, "", v)
  id = v; sub(/: .*/, "", id); sub(/.*\$/, "", id)
  sub(/^[^ ]+ /, "", v)
  if (!match(v, /[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+[0-9A-Za-z.+~_-]*/)) next
  v = substr(v, RSTART, RLENGTH)
  if (id == "electron") print sig "\t" file "\telectron\t" v
  else if (id == "rel") rel[sig SUBSEP file SUBSEP prod SUBSEP v] = 1
  else {{ print sig "\t" file "\t" prod "\t" v; versioned[sig SUBSEP file SUBSEP prod] = 1 }
}

END {{
  for (k in rel) {{
    split(k, a, SUBSEP)
    if (!((a[1] SUBSEP a[2] SUBSEP a[3]) in versioned)) {{ print a[1] "\t" a[2] "\t" a[3] "\t" a[4]; fromrel[a[1] SUBSEP a[2] SUBSEP a[3]] = 1 }
  }
  for (k in seen) {{
    split(k, a, SUBSEP)
    if (!(k in versioned) && !(k in fromrel)) print a[1] "\t" a[2] "\t" a[3] "\tunknown"
  }
}
AWK_SCRIPT

# ---- build list of files to scan ------------------------------------------
# yr has no "stay on one filesystem" option, so find builds the file list:
#  -xdev       don't cross into other mounts (DMGs, network shares, external
#              disks, autofs). The APFS Data volume is firmlinked into / with
#              the same device id, so /Users, /Applications etc. are included.
#  -type f     regular files only; symlinks are not followed
#  prunes      /System/Volumes (Data would be scanned twice via its firmlinks,
#              the rest are separate system volumes), /dev, and this script's
#              own output/work folders (old results contain matched strings),
#              plus the default skips described in the header
# Paths are resolved with pwd -P because /var, /tmp are symlinks to /private.
if [ "$(id -u)" -ne 0 ]; then
  log "WARNING: not running as root - protected files will be skipped (see log)"
fi
# A failed cd would make set -e exit 1, which means "matches found"
OUTPUT_DIR_REAL="$(cd "$OUTPUT_DIR" && pwd -P)" || {{ log "ERROR: cannot enter $OUTPUT_DIR"; exit 2; }
WORK_DIR_REAL="$(cd "$WORK_DIR" && pwd -P)"
if [ -d "$TARGET_PATH" ]; then
  TARGET_REAL="$(cd "$TARGET_PATH" && pwd -P)" || {{ log "ERROR: cannot enter $TARGET_PATH"; exit 2; }
else
  TARGET_REAL="$(cd "$(dirname "$TARGET_PATH")" && pwd -P)/$(basename "$TARGET_PATH")" ||
    {{ log "ERROR: cannot enter the folder of $TARGET_PATH"; exit 2; }
fi
TARGET_PATH="$TARGET_REAL"

# path_within PATH DIR: PATH is DIR or somewhere below it
path_within() {{ case "$1/" in "$2"/*) return 0 ;; esac; return 1; }

# An explicitly named file is always scanned
NONEMPTY=( -size +0c )
if [ -f "$TARGET_PATH" ]; then
  MAX_MB=0; SKIP_SEALED=0; SKIP_NONBINARY=0; SKIP_CLOUD=0; NONEMPTY=()
fi

if [ "$SKIP_SEALED" -eq 1 ]; then
  MACOS_MAJOR="$(sw_vers -productVersion | cut -d. -f1)"
  if [ "$MACOS_MAJOR" -lt 12 ]; then
    log "macOS $MACOS_MAJOR may have bundled PHP in /usr/bin - scanning system paths"
    SKIP_SEALED=0
  fi
  for d in /System /bin /sbin /Library/Apple /usr; do
    if path_within "$TARGET_PATH" "$d" && ! path_within "$TARGET_PATH" /usr/local; then
      SKIP_SEALED=0
    fi
  done
fi
if [ "$SKIP_CLOUD" -eq 1 ]; then
  case "$TARGET_PATH/" in
    */Library/CloudStorage/*|*/Library/Mobile\ Documents/*) SKIP_CLOUD=0 ;;
  esac
fi

PRUNE=( -path /System/Volumes -o -path /dev -o -path "$OUTPUT_DIR_REAL" -o -path "$WORK_DIR_REAL" )
if [ "$SKIP_SEALED" -eq 1 ]; then
  # find's -path * also matches "/", hence both /usr/local exclusions. Apple's
  # bundled Apache is kept: /usr/sbin and /usr/libexec are walked, but every
  # entry in them except httpd and apache2 is pruned
  PRUNE+=( -o -path /System -o -path /bin -o -path /sbin -o -path /Library/Apple
           -o \( -path '/usr/*' ! -path /usr/local ! -path '/usr/local/*'
                 ! -path /usr/sbin ! -path /usr/sbin/httpd
                 ! -path /usr/libexec ! -path /usr/libexec/apache2 ! -path '/usr/libexec/apache2/*' \) )
  log "Skipping sealed system paths (target one with -t, or -A, to include)"
fi
# -x: find prints resolved paths, so an existing folder is resolved like
# TARGET_PATH (/tmp -> /private/tmp). A skip that is or contains TARGET_PATH
# would prune the whole walk, so like the other skips it is turned off.
# ${{a[@]+"${{a[@]}"}: bash 3.2 (macOS) calls an empty array unbound under set -u
for d in ${{EXCLUDE_DIRS[@]+"${{EXCLUDE_DIRS[@]}"}; do
  if [ -d "$d" ]; then d="$(cd "$d" && pwd -P)"; fi
  # $d unquoted on purpose: it may be a pattern such as */node_modules
  # shellcheck disable=SC2254
  case "$TARGET_PATH/" in ${{d%/}/*) log "Not skipping $d (-x): it contains $TARGET_PATH"; continue ;; esac
  PRUNE+=( -o -path "$d" )
  log "Skipping $d (-x)"
done
DATALESS=()
if [ "$SKIP_CLOUD" -eq 1 ]; then
  PRUNE+=( -o -path '*/Library/CloudStorage' -o -path '*/Library/Mobile Documents' )
  # iCloud "Desktop & Documents" leaves online-only files outside those folders.
  # An unknown flag name is a fatal find error, so probe it first (older macOS).
  if find / -maxdepth 0 -flags +dataless >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    DATALESS=( ! -flags +dataless )
  fi
  log "Skipping cloud-drive folders and online-only files (target one with -t to include)"
fi
# Files at/over the cap are printed with a SKIPPED_SIZE tag, split out below
SIZE_TEST=( -print )
if [ "$MAX_MB" -gt 0 ]; then
  SIZE_TEST=( \( -size -"$((MAX_MB * 1048576))"c -print -o -exec printf 'SKIPPED_SIZE\t%s\n' {{} + \) )
  log "Skipping files of ${{MAX_MB} MB or more (use -m 0 to include)"
fi

SCAN_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/scan_list.txt"
FIND_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/find_list.txt"
# A newline (or other control character) in a path would break the one-path-
# per-line lists, so such paths go NUL-separated to their own list, copied below
CTRL_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/ctrl_list.bin"
: >"$CTRL_LIST"
log "Listing files under $TARGET_PATH (skipping other mounts)"
# shellcheck disable=SC2016  # $0/$@ are for the inner sh
nice -n 10 find "$TARGET_PATH" -xdev \( "${{PRUNE[@]}" \) -prune \
  -o -type f ${{NONEMPTY[@]+"${{NONEMPTY[@]}"} ${{DATALESS[@]+"${{DATALESS[@]}"} \
  \( -path '*[[:cntrl:]]*' -exec sh -c 'printf "%s\0" "$@" >>"$0" || true' "$CTRL_LIST" {{} + \
     -o "${{SIZE_TEST[@]}" \) >"$FIND_LIST" 2>>"$LOG_FILE" || true  # non-zero on permission errors

TAB=$'\t'
SIZE_SKIPPED="$(grep -c "^SKIPPED_SIZE${{TAB}" "$FIND_LIST" || true)"
if [ "$SIZE_SKIPPED" -gt 0 ]; then
  sed -n "s/^SKIPPED_SIZE${{TAB}/skipped (>= ${{MAX_MB} MB): /p" "$FIND_LIST" >>"$LOG_FILE"
  log "Skipped $SIZE_SKIPPED file(s) of ${{MAX_MB} MB or more (listed in $LOG_FILE)"
fi
if [ "$SKIP_NONBINARY" -eq 1 ]; then
  # Extension lookup in awk, ~3x faster than macOS grep -Eiv with a 100-way
  # alternation (20 s vs 64 s for 4.1M paths) and the same result
  awk -v exts="$NONBINARY_EXTS" '
    BEGIN {{ n = split(exts, a, " "); for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) skip[a[i]] = 1 }
    /^SKIPPED_SIZE\t/ {{ next }
    {{ f = $0; sub(/.*\//, "", f)
      if (match(f, /\.[^.]*$/) && (tolower(substr(f, RSTART + 1)) in skip)) next
      print }' "$FIND_LIST" >"$SCAN_LIST"
  NONBINARY_SKIPPED=$(( $(wc -l <"$FIND_LIST") - SIZE_SKIPPED - $(wc -l <"$SCAN_LIST") ))
  log "Skipped $NONBINARY_SKIPPED non-executable file type(s) (use -A to include)"
else
  grep -v "^SKIPPED_SIZE${{TAB}" "$FIND_LIST" >"$SCAN_LIST" || true
fi

# Control-character paths are scanned as ASCII-named copies in the work folder
# (a symlink loses the path in json output, a hard link would change the file's
# ctime), and their results show the bash $'...' quoted real path. They skipped
# the size cap and extension filter in find (a folder name with a tab sends
# every file below it here), so both are applied now.
COPY_MAP="${{WORK_DIR}/copy_map.txt"
: >"$COPY_MAP"
COPIES=0
CTRL_SKIPPED=0
NONBINARY_RE="\\.(${{NONBINARY_EXTS// /|})\$"  # same test as the awk filter above
shopt -s nocasematch
while IFS= read -r -d '' p; do
  if [ "$SKIP_NONBINARY" -eq 1 ] && [[ ${{p##*/} =~ $NONBINARY_RE ]]; then
    CTRL_SKIPPED=$((CTRL_SKIPPED + 1))
    continue
  fi
  if [ "$MAX_MB" -gt 0 ] && [ "$(stat -f %z "$p" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" -ge "$((MAX_MB * 1048576))" ]; then
    echo "skipped (>= ${{MAX_MB} MB): $(printf '%q' "$p")" >>"$LOG_FILE"
    CTRL_SKIPPED=$((CTRL_SKIPPED + 1))
    continue
  fi
  COPIES=$((COPIES + 1))
  copy="${{WORK_DIR_REAL}/copies/${{COPIES}_"  # trailing _ so .../1_ never matches inside .../10_
  mkdir -p "${{WORK_DIR_REAL}/copies"
  # An APFS clone (cp -c) takes no extra space; across volumes it fails, so
  # fall back to a plain copy
  if ! {{ cp -c "$p" "$copy" 2>/dev/null || cp "$p" "$copy" 2>>"$LOG_FILE"; }; then
    echo "unreadable (unusual name): $(printf '%q' "$p")" >>"$LOG_FILE"
    continue
  fi
  echo "$copy" >>"$SCAN_LIST"
  printf '%s\t%q\n' "$copy" "$p" >>"$COPY_MAP"
done <"$CTRL_LIST"
shopt -u nocasematch
if [ "$CTRL_SKIPPED" -gt 0 ]; then
  log "Skipped $CTRL_SKIPPED more file(s) with control characters in their paths (non-executable type or over the size cap)"
fi
if [ "$COPIES" -gt 0 ]; then
  log "$COPIES file(s) with control characters in their paths are scanned as copies; results show them as \$'...' paths"
fi
log "Found $(wc -l <"$SCAN_LIST" | tr -d ' ') file(s) to scan"

# ---- scan -----------------------------------------------------------------
log "Scanning with $THREADS thread(s) at low priority..."
log "Results: $RESULT_FILE"
SCAN_RC=0
# yr takes several [NAMESPACE:]RULES_PATH arguments before the target, so each
# signature file is loaded into its own namespace
nice -n 10 "$YR" scan --scan-list --print-meta --print-strings --threads "$THREADS" \
  --output-format "$OUTPUT_FORMAT" \
  "${{RULE_ARGS[@]}" "$SCAN_LIST" >"$RESULT_FILE" 2>>"$LOG_FILE" || SCAN_RC=$?

# find and yr report per-file errors (e.g. permission denied) on stderr; yr still
# scans the rest and exits 0, so non-zero means the scan itself failed
if [ "$SCAN_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
  log "ERROR: yr exited with code $SCAN_RC (see $LOG_FILE)"
  exit 4
fi

# Put the real (quoted) paths back in place of the copies
if [ -s "$COPY_MAP" ]; then
  awk -v map="$COPY_MAP" -v fmt="$OUTPUT_FORMAT" '
    BEGIN {{ FS = "\t"
            while ((getline line < map) > 0) {{
              i = index(line, "\t"); k = substr(line, 1, i - 1); v = substr(line, i + 1)
              if (fmt != "text") {{ gsub(/\\/, "\\\\", v); gsub(/"/, "\\\"", v) }  # JSON string escaping
              n++; from[n] = k; to[n] = v } }
    {{ for (j = 1; j <= n; j++)
        while ((p = index($0, from[j])) > 0) $0 = substr($0, 1, p - 1) to[j] substr($0, p + length(from[j]))
      print }' "$RESULT_FILE" >"${{RESULT_FILE}.tmp"
  mv "${{RESULT_FILE}.tmp" "$RESULT_FILE"
fi

if [ -n "$SUMMARY_FILE" ]; then
  # The map file is given twice: once as the first input file, once with -v so
  # versions.awk can tell it apart from the scan output by name
  awk -f "$VERSIONS_AWK" -v MAPFILE="$RULE_MAP_FILE" "$RULE_MAP_FILE" "$RESULT_FILE" |
    sort -u >"$SUMMARY_FILE"
  log "Versions: $SUMMARY_FILE ($(wc -l <"$SUMMARY_FILE" | tr -d ' ') line(s))"
  # Every signature that was loaded gets a line, matched or not, so the
  # analysis can tell "no match" apart from "signature never deployed"
  awk -F '\t' -v summary="$SUMMARY_FILE" '
    BEGIN {{ while ((getline line < summary) > 0) {{
               i = index(line, "\t")
               if (i > 0) n[substr(line, 1, i - 1)]++ } }
    {{ printf "%s\t%d\n", $1, n[$1] + 0 }' "$SIGNATURE_LIST" | sort >"$SIGNATURE_FILE"
  log "Signature counts: $SIGNATURE_FILE ($(wc -l <"$SIGNATURE_FILE" | tr -d ' ') signature(s))"
fi

if [ -s "$LOG_FILE" ]; then
  log "$(wc -l <"$LOG_FILE" | tr -d ' ') skip/warning/error line(s) in $LOG_FILE"
else
  rm -f "$LOG_FILE"
fi

# Any output in text/ndjson mode means at least one match; json always prints an object
if [ "$OUTPUT_FORMAT" = "json" ]; then
  grep -q '"rule"\|"identifier"' "$RESULT_FILE" && MATCHED=1 || MATCHED=0
else
  [ -s "$RESULT_FILE" ] && MATCHED=1 || MATCHED=0
fi

if [ "$MATCHED" -eq 1 ]; then
  log "MATCHES FOUND - see $RESULT_FILE"
  exit 1
fi
log "No matches."
exit 0
_END_OF_SCRIPT_
copy __createfile "{parameter "temp_folder"}/yara_x_scan_macos.sh"

// The wrapper that runs it and publishes the results into the engine results folder
delete __createfile
createfile until _END_OF_SCRIPT_
#!/bin/bash
# Started by the BigFix task with "run": runs the scan in the background, then
# publishes the summary (yara_results_runtimes.text) and the per-signature
# counts (yara_results_signatures.text) into the engine results folder, where
# the analysis "YARA-X Scan Results - Universal" reads both. scan.running marks
# a scan in progress (the task is not relevant while it exists).
T="{parameter "temp_folder"}"
D="{parameter "results_folder"}"
B="{parameter "bin_folder"}"
R="{parameter "signatures_folder"}"
touch "$T/scan.running"
/bin/bash "$T/yara_x_scan_macos.sh" -a "$T" -b "$B" -r "$R" -o "$T/results" >"$T/scan_console.log" 2>&1
rc=$?
echo "scan exit code: $rc (0 = nothing matched, 1 = matches found, 2 = bad arguments or no rules, 3-4 = error)" >>"$T/scan_console.log"
mkdir -p "$D"
# Only a finished scan replaces the results; written under a temp name first so
# the analysis never reads a partial file
if [ "$rc" -le 1 ]; then
  # Counts first: a reader that sees a fresh summary then always sees counts at
  # least as new
  for f in "$T"/results/yara_results_signatures_*.text; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    cp "$f" "$D/.{parameter "signatures_file"}.tmp" && mv -f "$D/.{parameter "signatures_file"}.tmp" "$D/{parameter "signatures_file"}"
  done
  for f in "$T"/results/yara_results_*.text; do
    case "$f" in *.raw.text) continue ;; esac
    case "$f" in */yara_results_signatures_*) continue ;; esac
    cp "$f" "$D/.{parameter "results_file"}.tmp" && mv -f "$D/.{parameter "results_file"}.tmp" "$D/{parameter "results_file"}"
  done
fi
cp "$T/scan_console.log" "$D/yara_scan_runtimes.log"
# The archive is prefetched again by every run; yr stays in the bin folder
rm -f "$T"/yara-x-v* "$T/scan.running"
_END_OF_SCRIPT_
copy __createfile "{parameter "temp_folder"}/run_scan.sh"

// Mark the scan as running before it starts, so the task isn't relevant again
// in the meantime (the wrapper removes the marker when the scan ends)
delete __createfile
createfile until _END_OF_SCRIPT_
started by action {id of action}
_END_OF_SCRIPT_
copy __createfile "{parameter "temp_folder"}/scan.running"

// Start the scan without waiting (it can take 10-30+ minutes): the action ends
// here and the client runs other actions. The results show up when it finishes.
run /bin/bash "{parameter "temp_folder"}/run_scan.sh"
]]></ActionScript>
			<SuccessCriteria Option="RunToCompletion"></SuccessCriteria>
		</DefaultAction>
	</Task>
</BES>
